\title[Perturbation theory on theory-space]{Perturbation Theory on Theory-Space:\\
A Meta-Theorem on the Unreasonable Effectiveness of Wrong Theories}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{16 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We develop a quantitative theory of \emph{approximate correctness} for physical
theories, in which a theory is an assignment of real predictions to phenomena and
its deviation from the truth is organised as a convergent power series in a
coupling parameter. Within this framework we prove a meta-theorem: for every
approximately correct theory and every accuracy threshold $\eta>0$ there is a
coupling window $|\eps|<\delta$, chosen independently of any competitor, on which
the approximate theory strictly outpredicts \emph{every} rival theory on the
entire class of phenomena where that rival's error is at least $\eta$. We show
this class is nonempty whenever the rival is inexact anywhere, and that
nowhere-exact theories are dense in theory-space, so the hypothesis is generic.

We then analyse the internal structure of wrongness. The tower of finite-order
truncations --- each knowingly wrong, discarding infinitely many corrections ---
is strictly ordered by predictive accuracy on a common punctured coupling
window: whenever the $M$-th correction is nonvanishing, every truncation of order
$N>M$ strictly beats the $M$-th, and orders $0,\dots,K$ form a strict chain. This
is the precise sense in which the wrongness of an approximately correct theory
forms a convergent series \emph{toward} the truth, monotonically in order and not
merely in the limit.

Both main theorems are proved sharp: an approximately correct theory at coupling
$\eps=1/2$ is beaten by a crude and itself-wrong constant rival, and a two-term
family at $\eps=1$ has its first-order truncation strictly worse than its
zeroth-order one. Finally we prove an \emph{epistemic half-space theorem} (the
set of worlds in which one prediction beats another is a nonempty open unbounded
half-line, so inferiority is never intrinsic), exhibit a Condorcet cycle showing
that majority empirical adequacy is not transitive, and construct a sequence of
theories, each wrong at every phenomenon, whose errors converge uniformly to
zero. Wilson's two-loop anomalous dimension is treated as a worked instance.
\end{abstract}

